/-!
# Conormal torsion for a one-dimensional completed component

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0AW3, `lemma-not-formally-catenary`,
the paragraph treating dimension one (blueprint: lem-per-one-dimensional-component-conormal).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace Ideal

/-- A positive power of a one-dimensional minimal component of a local domain's completion
vanishes at that prime, and a positive power of the extended maximal ideal kills its conormal
module. Here `Ideal.Cotangent` is the quotient of the ideal by its square as a module.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0AW3, `lemma-not-formally-catenary`, dimension-one
paragraph (blueprint: lem-per-one-dimensional-component-conormal).

Proof:
1. The completion B is Noetherian by `lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-noetherian` and local with
   maximal ideal mB by `lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-local-ring`. Its ideals are finitely
   generated by `def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring`.
2. The ring B_q is Noetherian by `lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian`. Minimality of q and
   `lem-weil-pbc-minimal-prime-localization-radical` give radical(0) = qB_q. Finite generation
   and `lem-weil-pbc-fg-ideal-radical-power` give n >= 1 with (qB_q)^n = 0. Extension commutes
   with ideal powers, so J = q^n has JB_q = 0.
3. The module M = J/J^2 is finite and killed by J. At a prime p not containing q, an element
   x of q outside p has x^n in J, so M_p = 0. At q, the fraction criterion from
   `def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime` and JB_q = 0 show M_q = 0. A prime strictly between q
   and the maximal ideal would give a length-two prime chain in B/q, contradicting
   `def-weil-pbc-krull-dimension-ring` and the dimension-one hypothesis. Every prime is below
   the maximal ideal. Thus the support of M is contained in the closed point.
4. By `lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-support-annihilator`, V(Ann M) is contained in that point.
   The radical intersection formula in `lem-weil-pbc-zero-locus-eq-iff-radical-eq` implies
   mB is contained in radical(Ann M), including when M = 0 and V(Ann M) is empty. Finite
   generation of mB and `lem-weil-pbc-fg-ideal-radical-power` give c >= 1 such that
   (mB)^c is contained in Ann M. This is precisely the second conclusion. -/
theorem exists_pow_conormal_torsion_of_one_dimensional_component
    {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A] [IsDomain A]
    (q : Ideal (AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A)) [q.IsPrime]
    (hq : q ∈ minimalPrimes (AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A))
    (hdim : ringKrullDim ((AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A) ⧸ q) = 1) :
    ∃ n c : ℕ, 1 ≤ n ∧ 1 ≤ c ∧
      (q ^ n).map
        (algebraMap (AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A)
          (Localization.AtPrime q)) = ⊥ ∧
      ((IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A).map
        (algebraMap A (AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A))) ^ c ≤
        Module.annihilator (AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A)
          (q ^ n).Cotangent := by
  sorry

end Ideal
